  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 241 AM EDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nThe long term will be a period of changing weather as we swing back \nfrom our first real taste of fall back to a more summer environment \nbefore once again moving back into fall weather. Despite some \ncomplex interactions between several moving parts, ensembles and the \nAI guidance are in pretty good agreement overall on how thing should \nplay out through the weekend into early next week.\n\nStarting Wednesday night into Thursday, surface high pressure will \nbe established just off the Carolina coastline. The moisture from \nthe remnants of Hurricane Polo will have merged with an upper level \nlow moving over the 4 corners region. The net effect over us from \nthis system will be to really \"pump\" the subtropical ridge aloft \nbefore gliding along the baroclinic boundary established across the \ncenter of the county and getting absorbed to the north of us with a \nstrong system moving across Canada and the Great Lakes. \nUnfortunately, this means unseasonably warm temps will be making a \nreturn Thursday, Friday, and maybe into Saturday, though more on \nthat. Moisture will steadily increase as it is pumped from the Gulf \nand tropics into the area by the aforementioned surface high off the \ncoast through Saturday, bringing dewpoints into the upper 60s and \n70s. Rain chances will pick up again on Friday where the CWA will be \nnear the right entrance region of a jet streak to our north and \ndeeper moisture will be present through the whole column. This may \nbe somewhat diurnal in nature, similar to a classic summer day.\n\nGoing into Saturday, most guidance shows a Tropical Storm Rachel in \nthe east Pacific as a hurricane stalling a bit to the south of the \nBaja Cal peninsula. This is pumping up a more western based ridge \nand sending a potent wave into the upper midwest that will be \nimportant Sunday and Monday. Surface high pressure is progged to \nmove over the far northern Appalachians sometime on Saturday. Some \nuncertainty here on how things will evolve, as this will position \nthe high in a way to allow for some CAD to slide down to the south, \nbut the high may not be strong enough and sit long enough for much \neffect here. Either way, the column looks to be nearly saturated \nsetting us up for widespread showers and storms. \n\nGoing into Sunday, upper level system moves across just to the north \nand brings strong frontal system into the area. Rain chances look \nbest across northern Georgia, though everyone should see something \ninto Monday as a front slides south. NBM probabilities of over 2\" of \nrain in 24 hours are hovering around 30-40% in north Georgia, \nshowing there is a decent signal for good rainfall with this system \neven this far out. Front will bring cooler temperatures in the area \nfor the beginning of next week.\n\nLusk",
